-- Q4717: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SFAC No. 8 (QC20-QC25), comparability enables users to identify similarities and differences across entities and periods. Changing the depreciation method mid-stream disrupts period-to-period trend analysis, directly impairing comparability and requiring ASC 250-10-45 disclosures.
Wrong (A): Verifiability (QC26) means independent observers could reach consensus on the measurement. The analyst''s difficulty stems from inconsistent methods, not inability to verify amounts.
Wrong (B): Timeliness (QC29) means information is available early enough to influence decisions. The depreciation change does not affect when information is reported.
Wrong (D): Understandability (QC30) concerns clarity of presentation. The change impairs cross-period consistency, not the clarity of the reported figures themselves.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4717;

-- Q4720: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 105-10-05 and SFAC No. 8 (QC8), financial information has predictive value if it can be used as input to processes employed by users to predict future outcomes. Prior-year revenue figures used to forecast future trends directly exemplify this forward-looking relevance component.
Wrong (A): Historical cost confirming an original purchase price illustrates confirmatory value under ASC 105-10-05 (QC9), not predictive value, because it validates a prior expectation rather than informing a forecast.
Wrong (B): An auditor verifying inventory counts demonstrates verifiability, an enhancing qualitative characteristic, not a component of relevance.
Wrong (C): Disclosing accounting policies in the notes supports understandability and comparability but does not itself illustrate predictive value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4720;

-- Q4722: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the FASB Conceptual Framework (SFAC No. 5, paragraphs 63-70), recognition requires the item to meet the definition of a financial statement element and possess a relevant attribute measurable with sufficient reliability. Both criteria must be satisfied before an item appears in the body of the financial statements.
Wrong (A): Being denominated in a foreign currency is not a recognition criterion; many recognized items are in the reporting currency, and foreign currency is a measurement consideration, not a gating requirement.
Wrong (B): Footnote disclosure and board approval are not recognition criteria under the Conceptual Framework; disclosure supplements recognized amounts but does not gate recognition.
Wrong (D): While materiality affects reporting, SEC authorization is not a recognition criterion under GAAP. Recognition depends on element definition and measurability, not regulatory approval.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4722;

-- Q4725: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the FASB Conceptual Framework (SFAC No. 5, paragraph 67b), current replacement cost is the amount of cash that would be required to acquire the same or an equivalent asset today. It reflects current market acquisition conditions for equivalent service capacity.
Wrong (A): Historical cost records the original purchase price at the transaction date and does not update for current market conditions, so it does not reflect what is currently required.
Wrong (C): Present value of future cash flows measures the discounted expected inflows or outflows, reflecting an income-based measurement rather than the current acquisition cost of an equivalent asset.
Wrong (D): Net realizable value represents selling price less completion and disposal costs, an exit-price concept from inventory accounting (ASC 330), not the entry-price replacement concept.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4725;

